pam-auth-update profile for pam_ssoossh (Debian, Ubuntu)
========================================================

`ssoossh` is a pam-auth-update profile. The package installs it to
/usr/share/pam-configs/ssoossh, where pam-auth-update can find it, and
**never enables it**: nothing in the package runs pam-auth-update, and the
profile carries `Default: no`, so a pam-auth-update run triggered by some
other package leaves it switched off.

Enabling it is a deliberate act:

  sudoedit /usr/share/pam-configs/ssoossh     # set your own server first
  sudo pam-auth-update                        # then tick "ssoossh browser approval"

READ THIS BEFORE YOU ENABLE IT
------------------------------

pam-auth-update writes into /etc/pam.d/common-auth, and common-auth is
read by every service that includes it -- including screen lockers,
display managers and polkit. pam_ssoossh prints an approval URL and waits
for someone to visit it. A screen locker has nowhere to print that URL and
nowhere to take the approval, so on a machine with a graphical session
this can leave a user unable to unlock their own screen.

That is why pam_ssoossh(8) says not to put the module in a shared include,
and why this profile exists but is not turned on for you. If your hosts
are servers with no graphical session, the trade is usually fine. If any
of them has a desktop on it, prefer the per-service fragments in
../pam.d/, which put the module only in the stacks you choose.

The profile is `Auth-Type: Primary` at priority 704 with
`[success=end default=ignore]`, which is the pam-auth-update spelling of
the `auth sufficient` line the per-service examples use: an approval ends
the stack successfully, and anything else -- denied, timed out,
cancelled, server unreachable -- falls through to the password prompt.

Removing it
-----------

  sudo pam-auth-update --remove ssoossh

Do that *before* removing the package if you enabled it. The package does
not call pam-auth-update on removal either, for the same reason it does
not call it on install.

RHEL, Alma, Rocky
-----------------

The rpm ships no equivalent, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished.

authselect is the RHEL mechanism, and it owns system-auth and
password-auth outright: after `authselect select`, /etc/pam.d/system-auth
and /etc/pam.d/password-auth are symlinks into /etc/authselect/, rewritten
from the profile on every `authselect apply-changes`. Those two files carry
the same shared-include problem as common-auth, for the same reason -- so
the module does not belong in them either way.

The part that decides the packaging is that authselect has no drop-in.
A profile's features are fixed by whoever wrote the profile: there is no
way for a third party to add `with-ssoossh` the way this directory adds a
pam-auth-update profile. The only ways in are a custom profile under
/etc/authselect/custom or a vendor profile under
/usr/share/authselect/vendor, and both are whole copies of a stock profile
that then stop tracking its updates.

A vendor profile in particular is not the rpm's version of the file in
this directory, and the difference is the whole point. The profile at
/usr/share/pam-configs/ssoossh does nothing until an administrator runs
pam-auth-update and ticks it. A vendor profile overrides the stock profile
of the same name, so an rpm dropping one in would shadow `sssd` on every
host already using it, taking effect at the next regeneration, with nobody
having opted in. Shipping nothing is what keeps the rpm to the same
bargain the deb makes.

Use the per-service fragments in ../pam.d/ on these distributions. They
put the module only in the stacks you name, which is what you want here
regardless of packaging.
